## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design per-tenant policy enforcement, guard-detection, and prompt rewriting with auditable provenance.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant policy registry and per-tenant constraints
- Guard-detection vs. prompt rewriting
- System guard injection and explicit privacy constraints
- Audit artifacts and drift/telemetry considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
const policy = {
  Tesla: { pii: true, retentionDays: 90 },
  Google: { pii: false, retentionDays: 365 },
  Plaid: { pii: true, retentionDays: 30 }
};
function guardAndRewrite(tenant, prompt) {
  const violations = [];
  const lower = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (lower.includes("password") || lower.includes("ssn")) {
    violations.push("PII-like content");
  }
  const rewritten = `[${tenant}] ${prompt} /* guard: ensure policy compliance */`;
  return { rewritten, violations };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evaluate determinism and drift for two tenants under evolving policies?  
- What tests would you add to catch policy-evading prompts without compromising latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Aims to gauge handling of ambiguous prompts with simple, maintainable logic—crucial for multi-tenant data workflows without heavy ML.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight keyword-based intent detection
- Ambiguity handling with clarifying questions
- Structured 2-step plans and module payloads
- Lightweight testing with sample prompts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo JS prototype for disambiguation harness
function detectIntents(text){
  const intents = {
    time: /(last|recent|past|last month)/i,
    region: /(region|country|geo)/i,
    users: /(users|accounts|people)/i
  };
  const hits = Object.keys(intents).filter(k => intents[k].test(text));
  return hits;
}

function disambiguate(text){
  const intents = detectIntents(text);
  if (intents.length > 1){
    return {ambiguous: true, options: ["summarize by region", "summarize by time"]};
  }
  if (intents.length === 1){
    return {ambiguous: false, plan: ["Fetch data", "Summarize"], payload: {module: intents[0]}};
  }
  return {ambiguous: false, plan: ["Interpret", "Respond"], payload: {module: 'default'}};
}

console.log(disambiguate("summarize recent activity"));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale the heuristic to handle multilingual prompts?
- What tests would you add to prevent regression when adding new intents?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design robust intent normalization across paraphrases for a data-ops assistant.

## Key Concepts

- Paraphrase invariance
- Intent normalization
- Lightweight test harness
- Deterministic outputs

## Code Example

```python
def parse_intent(prompt):
    t = prompt.lower()
    if 'count' in t and 'region' in t:
        return {'action':'count','fields':['region','date']}
    if 'show' in t and 'by' in t:
        return {'action':'summarize','fields':['segment','date']}
    return {'action':'unknown','fields':[]}

def test_stability(base_prompt, paraphrases):
    intents = [parse_intent(p) for p in [base_prompt] + paraphrases]
    first = intents[0]
    return all(i == first for i in intents)
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to multi-tenant safety contracts?
- How would you handle ambiguous parsings across dialects?
